WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.940
There is nothing in the world quite like Evo.

00:00:14.949 --> 00:00:20.110
No esports tournament, no sporting event, no competition can compare to it.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:24.660
After all, there’s no event in the world where everyone is a competitor.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.500
Where everyone starts off on equal footing.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.360
Where everyone gets a shot at glory.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:39.220
(Casting)

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.420
Evo is the crucible where champions are made, but going 0-2 isn’t a curse.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.580
It just means you have someone to root for.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:51.600
Because on championship Sunday, the person who beat the person who beat the person who beat you

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.300
is up there, fighting to win it all.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.640
The story of Evo is one of the competitors who show up every summer to prove their mettle.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.900
It’s the story of a community fighting for their existence.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.480
Fighting for the spirit of a community they all love.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.310
Evo didn’t begin as the massive, 10,000-person juggernaut of a tournament that it is known

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.400
as today.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.360
In the mid-’90s, the North American fighting game community was spread out across New York,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:35.920
Northern California, Southern California and the midwest, but players rarely competed outside

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.120
of their regions.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.920
Regional pride was strong though.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.940
Arcades made for intense, close-quarters competition and the best player in the arcade

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.460
was a respected figure.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.160
They were rulers of their domain, and always looking to expand their kingdom to other arcades.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.920
And everyone wanted to take their spot.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:59.140
"And you know if someone showed up to your arcade that you had never seen before, you guys

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.340
your crew had to make sure that they beat them because they were invaders and everything

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.140
like that and so there was just a lot of talk."

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.020
But people didn’t travel much for fighting

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.000
games back then.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.810
There was no professional scene, no significant
prize pools.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.820
Occasionally, one person went on vacation and ended up in another city’s arcade, and

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.060
then returned home to either talk trash or admit defeat.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.560
That led to a lot of interregional trash talk on early internet message boards, without

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.220
much of a way to settle the score.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.680
"And there was a lot of, well there was a lot of sh*t talking that went on right so."

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.340
At least not until Battle by the Bay.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:46.880
1996’s Battle of the Bay was a chance to prove which region reigned supreme.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.200
"The energy was pretty strong in there because it was really the first time that something like this

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.940
happened. There was a couple people who flew out from Kuwait, okay. But that was definitely very unique and

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.100
very surprising at the time."

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.819
The tournament was  a success, and inspired other, multi-regional

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.489
tournaments across the US.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:13.849
Evo co-founders Tom Cannon, Tony Cannon, Joey Cuellar and Seth Killian didn’t see the

00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:19.580
need for another Battle by the Bay until 2000 when they hosted the B4 championships.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.460
Held in Folsom near Sacramento, B4 was bigger in every way.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.040
but it was still held at a local arcade.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.500
"I just remember there being a ton of people. I remember the place being hot as hell.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.280
And I remember people just like chilling outside.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.320
like just talking it up and stuff."

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.640
This time, it wasn’t the entire scene that needed help, it was the West coast.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.040
Players from the East had the East Coast Championships as their mecca,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.160
and while the West Coast’s

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.500
massive player base made for plenty of smaller tournaments in the wake of B3, there wasn’t

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.920
anything big enough to be a Major.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.200
Then B5 hit the next year and the tournament grew again, bringing Japanese competitors

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.100
into the fray for the first time.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.859
Everyone was still in Sacramento, which gave the locals a chance to see players they dreamed

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.040
of playing against.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:14.280
"It was actually pretty unreal, like BAS I had heard of

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.000
BAS I had heard of for the first time in Alpha 3.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:24.380
And someone had mentioned that he was coming to B5. And I like immediately lost my sh*t."

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.120
The small handful of Japanese players that showed up dominated the games they competed

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:34.380
in which marked the beginning of the B-series’ reputation as The tournament series.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.720
No other US tournament had Japanese players in attendance.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:39.440
And that was a big deal.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.360
"I mean like I was saying before it was a huge deal when we'd heard that Japanese guys were coming

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.480
to compete at B5.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.380
I was just like man Japanese players play Marvel 2 too?

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:57.080
Like I knew they played the main line Street Fighter games. But like I wanna see these guys

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.540
in action for Marvel 2. And that sort of brought it's own little intrigue."

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:06.580
But the arcade scene that birthed the Battle by the Bay tournaments was dying, fast.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.000
By 2002, Southern Hills Golfland would close,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.520
and arcades all across North America were going out of business.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.360
The competitive environment that created fighting game communities all around the world was going away

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.020
in the states,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:25.340
and the crew behind B3, 4 and 5 wanted to find a way to preserve it.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:30.360
Enter the Evolution Championship Series, or Evo for short.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.580
Evo was meant to preserve the spirit of the arcade,

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.520
even if the arcades themselves were

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:36.460
going away.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.900
"We talk about arcade culture and some people think about racing games or skeeball or something

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.260
like that. That's not the part of arcade culture that we cared about.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.980
We care about like the tournaments that were happening, all over the country

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.920
And that we really love and wanted to save.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.720
And that's why we made EVO."

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.020
Though, it wasn’t always going to be called Evo.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.720
Plenty of names were through around, including Apex, Riot Zone and….

00:05:57.720 --> 00:05:59.040
FightCon.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:05.560
Evo 2002 was held in a UCLA campus in Pomona, and the organizers rented arcade machines

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.900
from local arcades.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.040
When they realized they’d only have enough machines for the tournament and none for free-play,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.680
they had to get creative with the event structure.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.100
"And we wanted to turn the event, not just into a tournament, but

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.580
turn it into a little bit of a show. So that's where Sunday comes from.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:29.440
Let's run all the tournaments, until the Top 8. And then we'll run the Top 8, The very best of the best,

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.280
back to back to back to back on Sunday. And it'll be a show, it'll be an experience, everybody will love it

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:34.400
and they'll stick around.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.700
And that's where the idea of like Top 8 comes from. It's literally that."

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:43.560
By Evo 2003, there were more than 300 people in attendance.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.580
It was easily the largest fighting game tournament ever, and the sheer amount of attendees was

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.560
getting harder and harder to manage.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.000
On top of that, the death of the arcade scene was beginning to impact Evo in other ways.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:03.800
"The more EVO scaled up to have more players participate in it. The more need you

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:10.420
have to make that sustainable. And because the game was already on console, it only made sense."

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.380
The move to consoles was controversial to say the least.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:19.639
If Evo was about preserving the spirit of the arcade, many assumed the hardware would

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:20.940
obviously follow.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.880
"The reaction to the console switch was not good.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.720
I mean it was almost universally reviled.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.100
You know likepeople were mad."

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:36.920
But the mid-2000s were something of a dark age for plenty of Japanese fighting games,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:41.340
particularly Capcom fighters, the backbone of Evo in its early years.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:47.200
And few console ports, if any, were considered “arcade perfect” for Capcom’s games.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.540
"At that point in time after third strike there just really wasn't much happening."

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:58.800
"I think that a lot of people forget that, and a lot of people just don't realise that that was reality

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.860
Where we really didn't feel like another new thing thing was coming out."

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:11.180
Evo’s move to consoles wasn’t smooth for the tournament, as Street Fighter 3: Third

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:16.099
Strike didn’t have a console port that was even close to tournament-ready, so the Evo

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.240
2004 3S tournament was run on arcade hardware.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.020
But the controversy was quickly swept under the rug when the tournament produced some

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.440
of Evo’s most memorable moments.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:33.320
In what has now become one of the most iconic esports moments of all time, Justin Wong and

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:39.360
Daigo Umehara, two titans in the scene, faced off in the losers’ finals of Evo 2004’s

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.720
Third Strike tournament.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.020
Justin took a commanding lead in the last round of the first game,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:52.240
and it seemed like he was going to go up a game in a key set.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:56.140
With Daigo’s back to the wall, Justin bet that Daigo couldn't

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.240
parry every hit of Chun’s Hoyoku-sen Super.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:05.120
He bet that Daigo couldn’t execute 15 frame perfect inputs in a row with all the pressure

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.600
in the world on him.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:08.960
He was wrong.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:27.080
Daigo perfectly parried and punished Justin’s super and won the round, then the game.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.480
"You know everyone kept talking about how great he is and everything like that,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.740
But the Daigo parry was that one moment where it happened.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.040
And it happened in front of a bunch of people and it was ridiculous."

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.600
Evo moment 37 was everything Evo was supposed to be.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:49.880
It was a display of pure skill, under immense pressure, with a crowd just inches away from the players.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.740
After that, Evo organizers and members of the fighting game community realized things like EVO moment

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:02.060
37, which went viral on a young YouTube, could turn Evo into an ambassador, helping to grow

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.440
the fighting game community.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.620
But Evo 2004 was also messy at times.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:26.060
In the SoulCalibur II Grand Finals, RTD and Mick played a pretty low-quality grand finals

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:27.880
and allegedly split the pot.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.700
"It was obvious from anyone who plays fighting games, they were playing different characters and were

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:33.120
playing at a very subpar level."

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:38.400
The incident led Evo to create their first collusion rule, yet another attempt to preserve

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.240
the spirit of true, arcade-era competition.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.640
"We should look into that for next year, cause we need to solve this problem."

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:50.500
2005 presented another opportunity for growth as the tournament moved from California to

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:54.440
Las Vegas thanks in part to a partnership with Major League Gaming.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.880
But the partnership wasn’t exactly equal.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:00.420
"At the time, EVO was bigger than MLG

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:06.560
in a way, and in fact, they kinda tagged along with us.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.680
You know it was kind of a big deal."

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:13.180
Evo and MLG split the venue, one half for Evo’s fighting games, and the other half

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:15.480
for MLG Halo and Smash tournaments.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.940
But Evo brought way more players to the tournament, leaving them without any space in their corner.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:24.460
Meanwhile MLG’s tournaments pulled in a quarter of what Evo did.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:25.780
It wasn’t pretty.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:32.200
"When Halo was at EVO. Oh my god they had Halo at EVO right in the middle of the ballroom.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:36.240
And after the Halo tournament, one of the Cannon brothers goes on the megaphone

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:43.400
and goes. "We'd like to congratulate Team whatever..." What was the team? "Team Liquid or some sh*t right?

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.840
Because they're the winners of the Halo tournament."

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:51.540
And right when he said that, everyone in the whole room boo'd the sh*t out of them."

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.780
And MLG has never partnered with Evo since.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:12:01.420
By 2006 Evo was big enough to get Capcom’s seal of approval, and even a Toyota sponsorship

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.080
that let the tournament go on the road with Evo East and Evo West.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:09.760
It also led them to include a racing game on the roster for the first, and last time.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:16.700
"When Toyota became a sponsorship they wanted at least, one ra- like something to do with driving

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:24.000
at EVO so. They wanted something and so they basically picked Mario Kart DS cause it was going to be an easy

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
way to add that in to the schedule.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:29.080
But at Evo East, the tournament hit a snag.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:33.840
EVO needed CRT TVs, since more modern TVs would introduce too much lag.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:38.740
But their supplier could only offer them LCD and Plasma TVs a day before the tournament

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:39.500
started.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:40.680
Things got dicey.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.660
Someone overheard the conversation about the TVs and immediately went to the Shoryuken

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:48.400
forums to say that the tournament was ruined before and that people should stay at home.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:54.500
The Evo organizers found a way, loading 32 CRT TVs into a truck in the middle of the night

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.060
and working to put on a good event.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.940
Or they were going to, until they realized the truck was broken.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:06.680
“Tony, who’s in the truck with Gilbert gets on the horn to me when we're about half way through.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:10.800
And says to me, "Uh Tom there's a situation with the truck."

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.360
I'm like "Okay? Is everything alright?"

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:18.920
He's like "Well? Gilbert tells me that uh, the clutch on the truck is blown out.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:25.720
and so that means that if this truck ever stops, uh it's not gonna start again until we fix it."

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:30.720
It's 3am we're on the highway, we're fifty miles from home, and the truck cannot stop.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.280
Right? It's like Speed,

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:37.600
So we get a few miles down the road ahead of them  and we get to the toll road,

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:42.120
and Seth is driving, and he rolls into the toll. And he rolls down the window.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:47.000
says, "There's a truck behind us, and it can't stop."

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:59.560
He's like, "It's not out of control, it's a safe situation. But the truck behind us, is not going to stop at this toll."

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:09.920
The tolls is like two dollars, he says, "Here's 20 dollars, this is for us, and the truck behind us."

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:13.840
And he doesn't even wait for a response he gives the twenty dollars and he just rolls up the window

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.880
he just like, there we go.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:23.840
They rolled into the toll, and they slowed down as much as they could. And as they're approaching the toll

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:29.080
Gilbert and the attendant sort of had this connection, where their eyes locked.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:34.080
And the attendant was like "Are you the truck?" And Gilbert is like, "Yes I'm the truck."

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:45.840
And they're getting closer and the gate raised. And they go through and you know we made it right?"

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:55.520
2001 marked the beginning of Justin Wong’s four-year Marvel vs. Capcom 2 Evo streak that

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:59.080
would go on to define Marvel at Evo for the rest of the decade.

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:04.440
Justin won every Evo MvC2 tournament between
2001 and 2004.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:10.640
After that, he won three more in 2006, 2008 and 2010, MvC2’s last year at the tournament.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:17.280
"Justin Wong takes it, first and last EVO Marvel vs Capcom 2 Champion."

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.560
"He seals it up."

00:15:18.579 --> 00:15:23.120
In that time though, plenty of fighters attempted to match Justin’s incredible run.

00:15:23.139 --> 00:15:29.910
In 2005, Duc Do, the first Evo MvC2 champion at B4, broke the streak by taking first place

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:31.900
with some unorthodox Spiral play.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:42.040
In 2007, despite Justin’s extraordinary comeback against Yipes in losers’

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:46.360
"Justin Wong, oh my god."

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:50.400
Yipes still managed to bring home  the win.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:01.360
In 2009, Sanford Kelly, long considered to be one of the East Coast’s finest Marvel

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:06.240
players brought down Justin in the Grand Finals to etch his own name into history.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:10.760
"Sanford Kelly, he finally wins."

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:17.340
But Justin’s performance at the final Evo MvC2 tournament in 2010 was something of a sign.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:22.860
History had spoken and it said that Justin Wong was the greatest Marvel 2 player to ever

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.180
touch the game.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:28.000
But though Marvel 2 and a variety of Street Fighter games were the backbone of Evo for

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:32.440
years, both games were starting to feel a little stale
by the mid-2000s.

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:42.220
The hype from a small community built around a dying genre and out-of-date consoles wasn’t

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:43.800
sustaining Evo.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:47.740
There were plenty of attempts to bring people into the tournament, including adding new

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:49.640
games and rotating out old ones.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.840
That’s how the story of Super Smash Bros. Melee at Evo began.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:58.440
Melee first appeared at Evo in 2007, with Ken taking first place,

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:08.520
cementing his status as the best Melee player in the world at the time.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:14.080
But 2007 was also the last time Melee would appear at Evo for the foreseeable future.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:19.380
In 2008, Melee was replaced by Brawl, which the tournament organizers ran with items on

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:24.840
and a bizarre stage selection process that led CPU, a complete unknown, to defeat Ken

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:26.160
in the grand finals.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:38.320
"CPU, your Smash Bros. Brawl EVO 2k8 champion. Make some noise."

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:45.360
The Brawl tournament soured plenty of Smash players on Evo, and neither game officially returned in 2009."

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.880
2007’s eight games gave way to just six in the next year, making it the smallest Evo lineup

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:54.100
since 2002.

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:59.520
In some people’s eyes, the shrinking crowds and smaller number of games, were all pointing

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:00.820
to one thing:

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:07.180
Evo was dying, and the arcade scene they were striving to preserve would die along with it.

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:13.320
"In 2008 I still remember at one point looking around the room and going, "I know like

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:20.120
almost everybody here. Like I'm not seeing new faces." It just felt like there was a peak at some point.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:25.700
And you know even the MvC 2  was the strongest game for a while. At that point Third Strike was the strongest

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:34.600
game but it just didn't feel. It just didn't have that same pizzazz to it I don't know I can't explain it."

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:36.820
But  there was a small glimmer of hope.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:42.560
At EVO 2008, Capcom gave fans a chance to play their first new fighting game in years:

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.040
Street Fighter IV.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:48.260
Street Fighter IV had been announced months before, and had been available in Japanese

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:53.640
arcades, but Evo 2008 was one of the first times that the North American FGC got their

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:56.480
hands on the game that could save their scene.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:01.080
Street Fighter IV was designed to be a return to form, but more importantly, it was the

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:05.180
first numbered Street Fighter game since 3S in 1999.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:09.120
It was proof that Capcom still cared about this community that was born out of Street

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.520
Fighter II all those years ago.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:15.620
In fact, Street Fighter IV was designed to play similarly to Street Fighter II.

00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:19.630
It removed some of the more complicated mechanics
introduced in the Street Fighter Alpha and

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:24.090
III in favor of more straightforward
play, meant to encourage lapsed fans back

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:24.980
into the fold.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.300
It worked.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:40.400
Evo went from a few hundred players in 2008 to a 1,000-person Street Fighter IV bracket in 2009.

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:43.300
It brought the scene back to life.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:49.800
"I remember almost kinda sitting in that chair, jaw on the floor, just eyes wide open."

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.560
"Did this really just happen?"

00:19:52.560 --> 00:20:00.000
"It was the most insane experience that I ever had and probably only the second time in my life

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.280
the first time being the Daigo parry moment, that I sat down and I was like, this could be a thing."

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:15.640
"As soon as 2009 happened I was like, I can't go I have to stay, I have to ride this out."

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:21.360
In a perfect twist of fate, Daigo played Justin Wong in the first ever Evo Street Fighter

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:26.400
IV Grand Finals, calling back to their famous showdown five years earlier.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:30.500
"It felt like it was, fate that brought them in the grand finals."

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:36.340
"And it felt like there was really no chance maybe for Justin to win but then he reset the bracket."

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:43.140
"And I still remember how crazy the audience was going and this was the biggest the audience crowd had

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:43.700
ever been."

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:51.760
(Casting)

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:56.860
Daigo’s win kicked off a new era for Evo, but perhaps more importantly, it kicked off

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.020
a new era for the FGC.

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:04.180
The quality of the match itself, the intensity of the set and the thrill of being part of

00:21:04.180 --> 00:21:08.600
a 1,000 player bracket kicked the tournament into high gear.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:13.260
Thanks in part to Street Fighter IV, Evo has never looked back.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:18.220
It’s grown, year over year, to unbelievable heights.

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:22.380
But Street Fighter isn’t the only reason Evo has gotten so huge.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:27.080
The tournament struggled through the hard times to come out the other side, to a world

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:30.620
where just one game didn’t have to carry the whole FGC.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:34.100
Evo 2009 was the biggest Evo yet.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:36.760
But they were just getting started.

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:44.420
"Hadouken!"

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:48.040
"Continue?"

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:49.040
"Nine!"

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:49.800
"Eight!"

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.920
"So big for Hungrybox right now this is his moment."

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:53.680
"Seven!"

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.060
"Six!"

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:55.980
"Five!"

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:00.660
"Oh my god! He takes it!"

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:01.540
"Four!"

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:02.780
"Three!"

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:03.800
"Two!"

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.120
"What are you standing up for!"

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:07.560
"Why! Why!"

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:08.860
"Oh my god!"

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:09.760
"One!"

